# Component Communication Patterns

## Context
- When implementing component-based entity systems
- When components need to interact with each other
- When maintaining loose coupling between components is important
- When designing event-driven gameplay systems

## Requirements
- Keep components decoupled using appropriate communication patterns
- Use event systems for indirect communication between components
- Implement clear interfaces for direct component interactions
- Avoid tight coupling between unrelated components
- Ensure component communication patterns are consistent across the codebase

## Examples

<example>
```typescript
// GOOD: Event-based communication between components
class HealthComponent implements Component {
  private entity: Entity;
  private currentHealth: number;
  private maxHealth: number;
  
  constructor(entity: Entity, maxHealth: number) {
    this.entity = entity;
    this.maxHealth = maxHealth;
    this.currentHealth = maxHealth;
  }

  takeDamage(amount: number): void {
    this.currentHealth = Math.max(0, this.currentHealth - amount);
    
    // Emit a Phaser event that can be listened to by other components
    this.entity.scene.events.emit('entityDamaged', this.entity, amount);
    
    if (this.currentHealth <= 0) {
      this.entity.scene.events.emit('entityDied', this.entity);
    }
  }

  update(time: number, delta: number): void {
    // Component update logic
  }

  destroy(): void {
    // Clean up any listeners if needed
  }
}

// Component listening for events from others
class EffectsComponent implements Component {
  private entity: Entity;
  
  constructor(entity: Entity) {
    this.entity = entity;
    
    // Listen for events from other components
    this.entity.scene.events.on('entityDamaged', this.onEntityDamaged, this);
    this.entity.scene.events.on('entityDied', this.onEntityDied, this);
  }
  
  onEntityDamaged(damagedEntity: Entity, amount: number): void {
    // Only respond if it's our entity
    if (damagedEntity === this.entity) {
      // Create damage effects
      this.createDamageEffect(amount);
    }
  }
  
  onEntityDied(deadEntity: Entity): void {
    if (deadEntity === this.entity) {
      this.createDeathEffect();
    }
  }
  
  createDamageEffect(amount: number): void {
    // Visual feedback for damage
  }
  
  createDeathEffect(): void {
    // Death animation logic
  }
  
  update(time: number, delta: number): void {
    // Component update logic
  }
  
  destroy(): void {
    // Important: Clean up event listeners
    this.entity.scene.events.off('entityDamaged', this.onEntityDamaged, this);
    this.entity.scene.events.off('entityDied', this.onEntityDied, this);
  }
}
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```typescript
// BAD: Tightly coupled components with direct references
class HealthComponent implements Component {
  private entity: Entity;
  private currentHealth: number;
  private maxHealth: number;
  private effectsComponent: EffectsComponent; // Direct reference
  private soundComponent: SoundComponent; // Direct reference
  
  constructor(entity: Entity, maxHealth: number) {
    this.entity = entity;
    this.maxHealth = maxHealth;
    this.currentHealth = maxHealth;
    
    // Directly fetching other components - tight coupling
    this.effectsComponent = entity.getComponent<EffectsComponent>('effects')!;
    this.soundComponent = entity.getComponent<SoundComponent>('sound')!;
  }

  takeDamage(amount: number): void {
    this.currentHealth = Math.max(0, this.currentHealth - amount);
    
    // Direct method calls to other components
    this.effectsComponent.createDamageEffect(amount);
    this.soundComponent.playSound('damage');
    
    if (this.currentHealth <= 0) {
      this.effectsComponent.createDeathEffect();
      this.soundComponent.playSound('death');
    }
  }
  
  // Other methods...
}
```
This example shows poor component communication with tight coupling between components. The HealthComponent directly depends on and calls methods of other components, making it impossible to use independently and difficult to test.
</example>

## Communication Patterns

1. **Event-Based Communication**:
   ```typescript
   // Publishing component
   this.entity.scene.events.emit('eventName', this.entity, additionalData);
   
   // Subscribing component
   this.entity.scene.events.on('eventName', this.handleEvent, this);
   
   // Always clean up in destroy
   this.entity.scene.events.off('eventName', this.handleEvent, this);
   ```

2. **Component Registry Lookups**:
   ```typescript
   // Get component only when needed, not stored as property
   update(time: number, delta: number): void {
     // Only when needed, look up other components
     const movementComponent = this.entity.getComponent<MovementComponent>('movement');
     if (movementComponent && this.shouldAdjustMovement()) {
       movementComponent.adjustSpeed(this.speedFactor);
     }
   }
   ```

3. **Message Passing with Command Pattern**:
   ```typescript
   interface Command {
     execute(): void;
   }
   
   // Component sends a command without knowing who handles it
   this.entity.sendCommand(new ApplyDamageCommand(10));
   
   // Entity distributes to appropriate component
   sendCommand(command: Command): void {
     this.components.forEach(component => {
       if ('handleCommand' in component) {
         (component as any).handleCommand(command);
       }
     });
   }
   ```

